#!/bin/sh
# Forced command for the backup key in /home/pi/.ssh/authorized_keys.
#
# Installed on the PI (not on the collector).  The line that uses it reads:
#
#   command="/usr/local/bin/trisonica-backup-shell",restrict ssh-ed25519 AAAA... alex@mm12-backup
#
# Three things happen here, and each one is a deliberate limit on what the
# homeserver is allowed to do to the station:
#
#   restrict          no pty, no port/agent/X11 forwarding, no user rc file.
#   rrsync -ro        the key can run rsync and nothing else, can only *read*,
#                     and can only see below /home/pi.  A homeserver that is
#                     later compromised cannot alter or erase a recording, and
#                     cannot get a shell on the roof.
#   nice              the sending rsync yields the CPU to the logger.
#
# CAUTION about the `ionice -c 3` below: on this Pi it does nothing.  The
# kernel is using the mq-deadline I/O scheduler (`cat
# /sys/block/mmcblk0/queue/scheduler` -> "[mq-deadline] kyber none"), and I/O
# priority classes are only implemented by CFQ and BFQ.  It is left in because
# it is free and becomes real if the scheduler is ever changed to BFQ, but it
# must not be relied on, and an earlier version of this file claimed protection
# it does not provide.
#
# What actually keeps the backup out of the logger's way is therefore:
#   - `nice -n 19`, which is real (CPU, and the compression is the CPU cost);
#   - the collector's --bwlimit, which caps the read rate directly and does
#     not depend on the scheduler honouring anything;
#   - hourly scheduling, so the steady-state transfer is ~4.5 MB of newly
#     appended CSV rather than a scan of the card.
#
# This matters because the card's fsync tail latency is what produces the
# sub-second stalls documented in the README, and the job runs beside a live
# 10 Hz recording.

exec nice -n 19 ionice -c 3 /usr/local/bin/rrsync -ro /home/pi
